---
import MenuEN from '../../../components/MenuEN.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import Analytics from '../../../components/Analytics.astro';
import Seo from '../../../components/Seo.astro';
import { blogPostingJsonLd } from '../../../i18n/jsonld.js';
import '../../../sass/general.scss';
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="UTF-8">
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Shrikhand&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <Seo page="blogDestinations" lang="en" type="article"
        title="5 new kitesurf destinations with (almost) nobody around in 2026"
        description="Tired of fighting for a wind window with 80 other kiters? Here are 5 kitesurf destinations on the rise in 2026, starting with Bilene, our lagoon in Mozambique."
        image="/images/1-spot/2.webp">
      <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(blogPostingJsonLd('blogDestinations', 'en', {
        headline: '5 new kitesurf destinations with (almost) nobody around in 2026',
        description: 'Tired of fighting for a wind window with 80 other kiters? Here are 5 kitesurf destinations on the rise in 2026, starting with Bilene, our lagoon in Mozambique.',
        image: '/images/1-spot/2.webp',
        datePublished: '2026-07-01',
      }))} />
    </Seo>
    <Analytics />
</head>
<body>
    <MenuEN page="blogDestinations" />

    <main>
        <div class="blog-hero">
            <img src="/images/1-spot/2.webp" alt="Two kiters alone on the vast Bilene lagoon, Mozambique">
            <div class="blog-hero-overlay">
                <span class="blog-date">July 2026</span>
                <h1>5 new kitesurf destinations with (almost) nobody around in 2026</h1>
            </div>
        </div>

        <article class="blog-article">
            <p>
                Eighty kites in the window, a launch negotiated meter by meter, your neighbor's lines brushing against yours… If that sounds like your last kite trip, here's some good news: in 2026 there are still spots with reliable wind where you can draw your own line without weaving through traffic. Here are 5 destinations on the rise — and why we think the first one on this list will stay unbeatable for a long time.
            </p>

            <h2>1. Bilene, Mozambique — our lagoon</h2>
            <img src="/images/1-spot/2.webp" alt="Bilene lagoon, kitesurfing on empty turquoise water" class="blog-img">
            <p>
                On our lagoon, over 90% of the days in the season (September to March) are rideable — now that foils exist, you really have to try hard to stay dry. The thermal wind usually picks up in the afternoon. Our all-time crowd record: 12 kiters on the water at the same time, and that was already a big day. Plan on a 3-hour taxi ride from Maputo, or a <a href="/en/blog/by-plane">direct flight by Cessna</a> — we wrote a whole article about it. Riders who come once tend to come back the next season; their videos are out there online, and the reviews on <a href="https://maps.google.com/?cid=16956463275848852616" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google</a>, <a href="https://www.facebook.com/profile.php?id=100063467443392&sk=reviews" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Facebook</a> and <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g1597322-d15360648-Reviews-Bilene_Kite_Center-Bilene_Gaza_Province.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TripAdvisor</a> all tell the same story: it's not just a lucky year.
            </p>

            <h2>2. Salinas del Rey, Colombia</h2>
            <img src="/images/blog/destinations/colombia.jpg" alt="Caribbean coastline in Colombia, La Guajira region" class="blog-img">
            <p>
                The spot has already hosted two GKA World Championship stops — and yet it's still nowhere near as busy as the classic Caribbean spots. In the Americas, it's probably the destination climbing fastest on the radar of kiters looking for something new.
            </p>

            <h2>3. Kalpitiya, Sri Lanka</h2>
            <img src="/images/blog/destinations/srilanka.jpg" alt="Empty beach at sunset in Kalpitiya, Sri Lanka" class="blog-img">
            <p>
                On the island's northwest coast, a two-hour drive from Colombo. Reliable wind, warm tropical water, and a region still largely untouched by the mass tourism that has taken over the south of the country.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Lassarga, Morocco</h2>
            <img src="/images/blog/destinations/morocco.jpg" alt="Desert coastline near Dakhla, Morocco" class="blog-img">
            <p>
                Just 10 km from an increasingly packed Dakhla, this small fishing village gets the same powerful winds and the same unspoiled desert scenery — minus the crowds. A bit like Dakhla fifteen years ago.
            </p>

            <h2>5. Pranburi, Thailand</h2>
            <img src="/images/blog/destinations/thailand.jpg" alt="Quiet beach in Pranburi, Thailand" class="blog-img">
            <p>
                Off the classic tourist trail, Pranburi keeps things simple: steady wind, a quiet beach, zero fuss. The low-key alternative to Thailand's already saturated spots.
            </p>

            <h2>So, see you on the lagoon?</h2>
            <p>
                These 5 destinations share the same luxury: wind, without the crowds. But let's be honest — on that front, we think Bilene is still unbeatable. Want to see for yourself? Come check out our <a href="/en/the-kite-center-in-bilene-mozambique">kite center</a> and our <a href="/en/prepare-your-trip-to-bilene-mozambique">trip preparation guide</a>.
            </p>

            <a href="/en/blog" class="blog-back">&larr; Back to blog</a>
        </article>
    </main>

    <Footer />
</body>
</html>

<style>
    .blog-hero {
        position: relative;
        width: 100%;
        height: 560px;
        overflow: hidden;
    }
    .blog-hero img {
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        object-fit: cover;
    }
    .blog-hero-overlay {
        position: absolute;
        bottom: 0;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        padding: 40px 20px;
        background: linear-gradient(transparent, rgba(0,0,0,0.7));
        color: white;
    }
    .blog-hero-overlay h1 {
        font-size: 2.4em;
        margin: 8px 0 0;
        text-shadow: 1px 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.6);
    }
    .blog-hero-overlay .blog-date {
        font-size: 0.9em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.1em;
        opacity: 0.9;
    }
    .blog-article {
        max-width: 750px;
        margin: 40px auto;
        padding: 0 20px;
    }
    .blog-article h2 {
        margin-top: 40px;
        font-size: 1.4em;
    }
    .blog-article p {
        font-size: 1.05em;
        line-height: 1.8;
        text-align: left;
        color: #444;
        margin-bottom: 15px;
    }
    .blog-img {
        width: 100%;
        border-radius: 8px;
        margin: 25px 0;
        box-shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
    }
    .blog-back {
        display: inline-block;
        margin-top: 20px;
        padding: 10px 20px;
        background: #4695fc;
        color: white;
        border-radius: 5px;
        font-weight: bold;
        transition: background 0.2s;
    }
    .blog-back:hover {
        background: #3578d8;
    }
    .blog-back:visited {
        color: white;
    }
    @media (max-width: 600px) {
        .blog-hero {
            height: 350px;
        }
        .blog-hero-overlay h1 {
            font-size: 1.6em;
        }
    }
</style>
